3f.png)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/7a/7a2d2aec43b7653bae77bdf4f315281d1d44865c9a6cb1b0c52db09f5de9c79a.png)" 13194139534377 17592186045417 false] [17592186166638 1071 "![](images/ba/ba6337d5108c3907631fbc09c254f89809546eb74850e2c87cb140ad185aa727.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186166638 1071 "![](images/cc/cc3969660197510f4c7abb016b6d4ed593b8eb69e59fc9b1396c4c1ddcc1e549.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139534377 17592186045417 false] [17592186166638 1074 "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n![](images/e0/e0566311c99934171da98a96e1bed902588698ac948f817cea20d98920e64581.png)\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_2\\|=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} x_2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution ![](images/e6/e6415e2bc0b61c20486c61216ca322e3bdc0e6efb9ae2570c2b255b898225432.png) Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/f0/f053fd32c444f585085929ac6908e1d2bc66fb4dad71bdf64c6498a026f9d237.png)" 13194139534377 17592186045417 false] [17592186166638 1074 "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=2\\sqrt{2}$ 