=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186420426 1072 #uuid "3bcf1a6d-a909-52ae-9cf4-756772c84414" 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186420426 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-5I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186420427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420425 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186420427 1069 "q-96276" 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186420427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186420427 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186420427 1072 #uuid "ef95dbfa-6f61-5c3c-95a1-84a02502e472" 13194139534377 17592186045417 true] [17592186420427 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534377 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-21d874a6-42d2-5f2f-aa9a-a2fdf86c4b8e" 17592186420414 "answer-231de894-d699-5af8-a001-ea543d0bce13" 17592186420415 "answer-4e7e5095-8798-560f-9801-d4fa434e87b5" 17592186420416 "answer-614be16c-5f8b-58f4-b40e-2b97cb12c8ad" 17592186420417 "answer-839ddc42-b4ef-597f-9e89-6006c2f2540f" 17592186420418 "answer-949fcce7-9c5b-52bc-8db0-79ebc8184a34" 17592186420419 "answer-9aeafff4-018b-54ac-8bab-6f9acf422f59" 17592186420420 "answer-ded27d66-5795-5b7d-a3dd-9bc02a991b0c" 17592186420421 "answer-e816eb9d-759a-506a-a72a-536bf3e1d0c0" 17592186420422 "answer-f82696be-398b-59c4-b4cd-60c5e5db858f" 17592186420423 "answer-field-41bc2323-b376-5455-ae20-e3e95aae6c8a" 17592186420424 "answer-field-794b4e0b-ceb5-5019-9027-b26983813246" 17592186420425 "question-3bcf1a6d-a909-52ae-9cf4-756772c84414" 17592186420426 "question-ef95dbfa-6f61-5c3c-95a1-84a02502e472" 17592186420427}}
